Actually, this is something that could be leveraged to provide a
little more class distinction.  There are a number of classes -
Caveman, Barbarian, Valkerie, Rogue, and Samurai - that are clearly
not meant to be spell casters (or even literate).  Yet they are able
to write magical scrolls with perfection and can recite and invoke
them perfectly.  Prehaps making scroll reading and writing less
reliable for these classes would be something of an advantage.  This
could be a significant chance of failure, and/or lower maximum
enchantment limits.

Could they solve the problem by just throwing more scrolls of
enchantment at items?  Firstly, lower maximums are just that -
maximums - and secondly, they'd probably be busy trying to get a
Scroll of Identify to work.  The upshot is that they'd mostly be
playing with just what they found with minimal additional
enchantments... Oh, and rather than being granted spells books, gods
could give out random equipment enchantments for these classes.

I suspect it would leaves Knights and Clerics as the only heavy armour
class that could actually produce decent enchanted weapons and armour
- something that might increase thier popularity.  To over enchant
elven armour you might have to be an Elf, and likewise with Dwarven
armour.  Orcs are not known for thier spell casting abilities (and for
the most part, neither are Dwarves, but Dwarves are said to be good
makers of weapons and armour).

Wizards might even get a bonus on thier enchantment ability, letting
them get even higher than normal maximums (they can wear most armour,
just not big, heavy suits unless they've found GoP).

Tourits and Rangers would be dabblers - peanalised a little bit, but
not as heavily as the traditionally illeterate classes.

